   "versionNonce": 1955997541,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "Facts that keep this safe:",
   "fontSize": 17,
   "fontFamily": 5,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "baseline": 15,
   "containerId": null,
   "originalText": "Facts that keep this safe:",
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "e2d96a18ce4c",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 60,
   "y": 226,
   "width": 920.08,
   "height": 210.0,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 1,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 835092973,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 1649174967,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "F1  the commit frontier is a PREFIX: the batch at k+1..k+6 cannot commit while k is unfilled \u2014 the\n     cascade commits in order, so 'commit with a gap' is unrepresentable, not merely unlikely.\nF2  the old leader cannot even SEE the gap as committed \u2014 it just stalls; no decision is taken on k+1.\nF3  after crashes, a NEW leader runs ordinary VRR view sync: the fuse quorum at k+1..k+6 is carried\n     as prepared evidence; slot k is filled eventually \u2014 by the old leader's value if any quorum saw it,\n     else by the new leader's proposal (possibly a no-op) under the NEW view.\nF4  when k commits, the cascade resumes: k+1..k+6 fold as the ONE establishing batch they are.\nF5  the batch is a sequence of COMMANDS applied to the config with validation \u2014 not absolute weights \u2014\n     so a late-committing batch under a bumped era is still meaningful. A group naming identities no\n     longer present is dropped and logged as an error (operator-visible, not silent).\nF6  strong consistency = every node applies the SAME groups at the SAME slots; each successful\n     application bumps the era. Era is therefore a function of the committed prefix, identical everywhere.",
   "fontSize": 14,
   "fontFamily": 5,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "baseline": 12,
   "containerId": null,
   "originalText": "F1  the commit frontier is a PREFIX: the batch at k+1..k+6 cannot commit while k is unfilled \u2014 the\n     cascade commits in order, so 'commit with a gap' is unrepresentable, not merely unlikely.\nF2  the old leader cannot even SEE the gap as committed \u2014 it just stalls; no decision is taken on k+1.\nF3  after crashes, a NEW leader runs ordinary VRR view sync: the fuse quorum at k+1..k+6 is carried\n     as prepared evidence; slot k is filled eventually \u2014 by the old leader's value if any quorum saw it,\n     else by the new leader's proposal (possibly a no-op) under the NEW view.\nF4  when k commits, the cascade resumes: k+1..k+6 fold as the ONE establishing batch they are.\nF5  the batch is a sequence of COMMANDS applied to the config with validation \u2014 not absolute weights \u2014\n     so a late-committing batch under a bumped era is still meaningful. A group naming identities no\n     longer present is dropped and logged as an error (operator-visible, not silent).\nF6  strong consistency = every node applies the SAME groups at the SAME slots; each successful\n     application bumps the era. Era is therefore a function of the committed prefix, identical everywhere.",
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
